Now I understand the current design. Let me plan the work. I'll do auth first (bigger risk), then homepage modernization.

Let me start with the DB client. I'll create `src/lib/db/client.ts`:

```ts
import { getCloudflareContext } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare";

export interface DBLike {
  prepare(sql: string): PreparedLike;
}
```

Actually, D1Database has this type. Let me define a minimal interface that both D1Database and my node:sqlite shim satisfy:

```ts
export interface D1PreparedLike {
  bind(...values: unknown[]): D1PreparedLike;
  first<T = unknown>(): Promise<T | null>;
  all<T = unknown>(): Promise<{ results: T[] }>;
  run(): Promise<{ success: boolean; meta?: unknown }>;
}
export interface DBLike {
  prepare(sql: string): D1PreparedLike;
}
```

D1's D1Database.prepare returns D1PreparedStatement with bind/first/all/run — structurally compatible? D1's `first<T>()` returns `Promise<T | null>`. `all<T>()` returns `Promise<D1Result<T>>` which has `.results`. `run()` returns `Promise<D1Result>` with `.success`. So structurally yes.

But for type compat with the actual D1Database type, I can type `getDB()` return as `DBLike` and internally use D1Database which satisfies it.

Now the local shim using node:sqlite:

```ts
import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";

class NodeSqliteStatement {
  constructor(private stmt: StatementSync) {}
  bind(...values) { this.stmt = ... } // node:sqlite StatementSync doesn't have bind chaining; prepare with params
}
```

node:sqlite API: `db.prepare(sql)` returns `StatementSync`, and you call `stmt.run(...params)`, `stmt.get(...params)`, `stmt.all(...params)`. There's no `.bind()` chaining. So my shim needs to hold params and bind at execution time. 

```ts
class NodePrepared implements D1PreparedLike {
  private params: unknown[] = [];
  constructor(private db: DatabaseSync, private sql: string) {}
  bind(...values: unknown[]): this { this.params = values; return this; }
  async first<T>(): Promise<T | null> {
    const row = this.db.prepare(this.sql).get(...this.params) as T | undefined;
    return row ?? null;
  }
  async all<T>(): Promise<{ results: T[] }> {
    const rows = this.db.prepare(this.sql).all(...this.params) as T[];
    return { results: rows };
  }
  async run() {
    const res = this.db.prepare(this.sql).run(...this.params);
    return { success: true, meta: res };
  }
}
```

Note: node:sqlite `run()` returns `{ changes, lastInsertRowid }`. Good.

Local DB file: where? Put in `.data/local.sqlite` (gitignored). On startup, if the file DB is empty (no users table), create tables from `db/migrations/0001_schema.sql`? Parsing the migration SQL to apply via node:sqlite: the migration contains multiple statements. `DatabaseSync.exec()` can run multiple statements! node:sqlite's `db.exec(sql)` executes all statements in the string. So I can read the migration file and `exec` it. But it contains `PRAGMA foreign_keys` and INSERT OR IGNORE — fine.

Actually the migration file has a header comment and multiple statements — exec should handle it. Let me use `db.exec(contents)`.

Also: should local dev DB mirror the exact same schema? Yes, use the migration file. Also need `d1_migrations` bookkeeping? Not necessary for local shim.

Detection of Cloudflare vs Node:
```ts
export async function getDB(): Promise<DBLike> {
  if (isCloudflare()) {
    const ctx = getCloudflareContext();
    return ctx.env.DB;
  }
  return getLocalDB();
