parts.text:
Now the seed script. `scripts/seed-admin.mjs`:
- Generates PBKDF2 hash with node:crypto matching auth.ts format: `pbkdf2$<saltB64>$<derivedB64>` using 100000 iterations SHA-256, 256 bits (32 bytes).
- Uses node:sqlite to open `.data/local.sqlite` and upsert the admin user (email from args/env or default admin@aitech.media, password from arg/env or default).
- Prints SQL and wrangler commands for local + remote D1.

Wait — the local sqlite file may not exist yet if dev server hasn't run. The seed script should create it and apply the migration like local-sqlite.ts does. I'll replicate that logic in the script (read db/migrations/0001_schema.sql and exec).

But careful: if the file exists with tables already, migration exec would run again → CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS is safe; INSERT OR IGNORE seed categories safe. So I can just exec the whole migration every time. Actually simpler: always ensure schema by exec'ing migration (idempotent). Then upsert admin.

Upsert: 
```sql
INSERT INTO users (id, email, name, password_hash, role) VALUES (?,?,?,?,'admin')
ON CONFLICT(email) DO UPDATE SET password_hash=excluded.password_hash, name=excluded.name, role='admin', banned_at=NULL
```
SQLite supports ON CONFLICT. D1 too.

The script accepts: `node scripts/seed-admin.mjs [email] [password]`. Defaults: admin@aitech.media / (generate?) Let me require password or default. The user asked me to create one. I'll default password to something and tell them. Let me use a clear default: `Aitech-2026!` hmm. I'll generate a random-ish but memorable one: `admin_2026_secure`. Let me just pick `Aitech@2026` and display it prominently. Actually since I'll tell the user the credentials, memorable is better: email `admin@aitech.media`, password `Admin2026!`.

Let me also output the wrangler SQL commands. For remote, the user needs to run with the hash — the script prints a full command with the generated hash quoted properly. The hash contains `$` and `/`, `+`, `=` — in PowerShell double quotes, `$` needs escaping. That's a pain for copy-paste. Alternative: the script writes the SQL to a temp file `db/seeds/admin.sql` and prints instructions to run `wrangler d1 execute aitech-db --remote --file=db/seeds/admin.sql`. That avoids shell-escaping. And for local D1: `wrangler d1 execute aitech-db --local --file=db/seeds/admin.sql`. I'll have the script write `db/seeds/admin.sql` (gitignored? it contains a hash, not a password — safe-ish, but contains email; let me gitignore db/seeds/). Actually the SQL contains a password hash — fine to keep but let me gitignore it anyway and note it.

But wait — the seed script also needs to apply the admin to the wrangler-local D1 DB too so `wrangler dev` sees the admin. I can run the generated admin.sql against both:
- `.data/local.sqlite` via the script itself
- `wrangler d1 execute aitech-db --local --file=db/seeds/admin.sql`

And the user runs remote.

Let me write the script. It needs ESM (.mjs) since package.json has no "type": "module". Actually Next projects often have no type module. Use .mjs.

The script:
```js
import { createHash, randomBytes, pbkdf2Sync } from "node:crypto";
import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";

const root = process.cwd();
const email = (process.argv[2] || "admin@aitech.media").trim().toLowerCase();
const password = process.argv[3] || "Admin2026!";

const salt = randomBytes(16);
const derived = pbkdf2Sync(password, salt, 100000, 32, "sha256");
const hash = `pbkdf2$${salt.toString("base64")}$${derived.toString("base64")}`;
const id = randomUUID(); // node:crypto randomUUID

// ensure local schema
const dir = path.join(root, ".data"); mkdirSync(dir, {recursive:true});
const file = path.join(dir, "local.sqlite");
const fresh = !existsSync(file);
const db = new DatabaseSync(file);
db.exec("PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;");
if (fresh || true) { // always exec — idempotent
  const schema = readFileSync(path.join(root, "db/migrations/0001_schema.sql"), "utf8");
  db.exec(schema);
}
// upsert
db.prepare("INSERT INTO users (id,email,name,password_hash,role) VALUES (?,?,?,?,'admin') ON CONFLICT(email) DO UPDATE SET name=excluded.name, password_hash=excluded.password_hash, role='admin', banned_at=NULL").run(id, email, "Site Admin", hash);

// write admin.sql for D1
const sql = `INSERT INTO users (id, email, name, password_hash, role) VALUES ('${id}', '${email}', 'Site Admin', '${hash}', 'admin') ON CONFLICT(email) DO UPDATE SET name=excluded.name, password_hash=excluded.password_hash, role='admin', banned_at=NULL;\n`;
const seedsDir = path.join(root, "db/seeds");
mkdirSync(seedsDir, {recursive:true});
writeFileSync(path.join(seedsDir, "admin.sql"), sql);

console.log(`
✅ Admin user seeded locally (.data/local.sqlite)
   Email:    ${email}
   Password: ${password}

To apply the same admin to Cloudflare D1:
   Local (wrangler dev):   npx wrangler d1 execute aitech-db --local  --file=db/seeds/admin.sql
   Remote (production):    npx wrangler d1 execute aitech-db --remote --file=db/seeds/admin.sql
`);
```

Wait: `db.exec(schema)` every run — schema has `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`, `INSERT OR IGNORE`. Idempotent. Good. But there's also a subtlety: `ON CONFLICT(email) DO UPDATE` in the seed vs `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO categories` — fine.

Note: SQLite `ON CONFLICT(email) DO UPDATE` requires unique index on email — users.email has UNIQUE. Good.

`randomUUID` import from node:crypto: `randomUUID` exists. Good.

Also note: in the admin.sql hash contains `$` and `/`,`+`,`=` — fine inside a SQL file (no shell).

Now package.json: add script `"seed:admin": "node scripts/seed-admin.mjs"`. And bump @types/node to ^24.

Let me write the script and update package.json, gitignore (.data/, db/seeds/).

Then npm install @types/node@^24.

Then run seed, then test.

Also need to make sure admin users page `created_at` format parses in `new Date(...)` — D1 strftime gives "2026-08-10T..." which parses. In local sqlite same. Good.

Let me also double check `cookies()` import: `import { cookies } from "next/headers"` — in Next 16 cookies is async. In auth.ts I call `await cookies()`. Good.

Potential issue: `redirect()` imported in auth.ts from "next/navigation" — used in requireRole. In a route handler (API), redirect() throws NEXT_REDIRECT — only admin layout uses requireRole, fine.

Another: in auth.ts `sessionCookieOptions().secure` uses process.env.NODE_ENV — under workerd, process.env.NODE_ENV is set by OpenNext to production. Good.

Now, one concern about getSession being called during static generation: admin layout has `export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"` — that forces dynamic rendering for /admin routes. But do sub-pages (admin/articles etc.) inherit the layout's dynamic? `export const dynamic` in a layout applies to all pages under it. Yes — route segment config in layouts applies to child segments. Good.

Also the login page: server component importing LoginForm (client) — fine, page remains static.

Let me also consider: `/api/auth/*` route handlers — they're not under the admin layout, no gate. Good. Login/register/logout endpoints are public. `me` returns session or null — public.

Security: register allows anyone to create reader accounts — intended.

Now write seed script.

