    publishedAt: "2026-07-28T09:00:00Z",
    image: {
      cover: "generic",
      alt: "Rendering strategies comparison",
      src: "/images/articles/ssr-ssg-or-static-2026.svg",
    },
    seo: {
      title: "SSR vs SSG vs static rendering in 2026",
      description:
        "How to choose between static generation, server-side rendering and on-demand rendering for a fast, indexable site in 2026. Decision tree included.",
    },
    related: ["best-seo-tools-2026", "cursor-vs-copilot-vs-claude-code"],
    sources: [
      { label: "Next.js — rendering docs", url: "https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/rendering" },
    ],
    content: [
      {
        type: "p",
        text: "Every few years the rendering debate resets, and 2026's version is the most confusing yet: static generation, server-side rendering, incremental regeneration, cache components, and 'just use a CDN'. The good news is the underlying rule hasn't changed — render as much as possible at build time, render the rest as late as it needs to be.",
      },
      { type: "h2", text: "The decision tree" },
      {
        type: "list",
        ordered: true,
        items: [
          "Does the page exist before a user asks for it? Build it at deploy time — static generation (SSG).",
          "Does it change only when your team publishes? Build once, rebuild on publish — that's still SSG with on-demand revalidation.",
          "Does it depend on the visitor (auth, personalization)? Render at request time — server-side rendering (SSR).",
          "Is it genuinely unique per user, like an editor? Ship a client-rendered island inside a static shell.",
        ],
      },
      { type: "h2", text: "Where teams go wrong" },
      {
        type: "p",
        text: "The most common failure in 2026 is over-dynamic: every page gets SSR because 'we might need it later'. That's how you end up paying origin fees for content that never changes. The second failure is the opposite — caching a personalized page publicly and leaking sessions. Cache the shell, not the user.",
      },
      {
        type: "table",
        columns: ["Strategy", "Best for", "Watch out for"],
        rows: [
          ["SSG + on-demand rebuild", "Articles, docs, product pages", "Rebuild triggers"],
          ["SSR", "Auth, dashboards, price lookups", "Origin cost, latency"],
          ["ISR / cache components", "News sites, ranking pages", "Revalidation timing"],
          ["Client components in a static shell", "Editors, tool UIs", "SEO of the shell only"],
        ],
      },
      {
        type: "callout",
        tone: "info",
        title: "This site is a case study",
        text: "AI Tech ships as a statically generated Next.js site — every article is pre-rendered at build time, then pushed to the Cloudflare CDN. New articles trigger a rebuild. That's the entire infrastructure, and it's fast enough for a news cycle.",
      },
    ],
  },
];

