Line 81-83 in globals.css is the problem! 

```css
--font-display: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif);
```

This is a self-referential CSS custom property! `--font-display` is defined as `var(--font-display, fallback)`. Since `--font-display` is already defined by next/font's variable on the html element... wait. Actually next/font sets `--font-display` on the html element (the `.space_grotesk_..._variable` class defines `--font-display: 'Space Grotesk', 'Space Grotesk Fallback'` etc.). Then globals.css redefines `--font-display` on `:root` (or `html`) as `var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif)`.

A custom property referencing itself is invalid at computed-value time — `var(--font-display)` inside the definition of `--font-display` creates a cycle. Per CSS spec, a cycle makes the property invalid (guaranteed-invalid → unset/inherited). So `--font-display` at computed time becomes guaranteed-invalid, and `.font-display { font-family: var(--font-display) }` would resolve to... the inherited value? No — when var() references an invalid-at-computed-value-time custom property, the var() is replaced with the property's guaranteed-invalid value, making font-family invalid at computed-value time, which for font-family means it falls back to inherit? Actually invalid at computed-value time for a shorthand/property → the property becomes its inherited value (or initial for non-inherited). font-family is inherited, so it inherits from parent → which is body's font-sans (Inter). So display headings would show Inter, not Space Grotesk!

Wait, but is it a cycle? The next/font class sets `--font-display` (on html via the variable class). globals.css line 81 sets `--font-display: var(--font-display, ...)` on `:root`? Let me check what selector line 81 is under. Let me look at the globals.css around lines 75-95.

Actually the self-reference cycle: `:root { --font-display: var(--font-display, fallback) }`. Since both declarations are on the same element (html), the cascade picks one. The `:root` in globals.css has higher specificity? Both target html/:root. Specificity of `:root` is (0,1,0), specificity of the next/font class `.space_grotesk__variable` is (0,1,0) too — same specificity, so source order wins. globals.css is imported before or after? In layout.tsx, `import "./globals.css"` is line 3, fonts are loaded via next/font and the class applied on html. The font variable classes are applied on the html element class attribute, so they generate a stylesheet with those classes. The order of stylesheets is determined by Next's injection. This is ambiguous, but regardless — if globals.css `:root` wins, we get the self-referential cycle → invalid → font falls back to inherited (Inter for font-sans on body... wait, .font-display on elements inherits from body which is font-sans). Actually font-family is inherited, so if `.font-display` computes to invalid, it inherits font-sans (Inter). That explains "шрифта не става" — the display font isn't applying; everything shows Inter (which is basically default sans).

Hold on — but body uses `font-sans` which is Inter anyway, so the whole site looks uniform (Inter everywhere), and Space Grotesk headings don't render. Actually if Space Grotesk is the display font and it's broken, headings show Inter. The user says "шрифта че не става" — font doesn't work. This is very likely it.

Wait, let me reconsider. Actually next/font variable classes apply directly to `<html>` element. globals.css `:root` selector — in CSS, `:root` matches html element. So both rules target the same element. The winner depends on stylesheet order and specificity. `.space_grotesk__variable` is a class (0,1,0); `:root` is a pseudo-class (0,1,0). Equal specificity → source order. Next injects font stylesheets typically in the head via next/font (preloaded @font-face + the variable class definitions). globals.css is also injected. Order is uncertain. But if globals.css wins OR loses, there's a problem either way:

- If `:root { --font-display: var(--font-display, ...) }` wins: cycle → invalid → falls back.
- If next/font class wins: then `--font-display` is properly defined with the real font stack, and `.font-display` works.

But wait — actually maybe next/font's class does NOT define `--font-display` directly but uses `@property`? No, it defines CSS vars in the class. Hmm. But the fact the user reports the font broken strongly suggests the cycle theory (globals.css `:root` wins due to ordering — Next injects page CSS before/after; in dev, globals.css may be injected later than font CSS? unclear).

Also there's another subtle issue: `body` uses `font-sans` → `var(--font-sans)` which in globals.css is `var(--font-sans, "Inter", ...)` — same self-reference cycle for `--font-sans` and `--font-mono`! So body font-family → invalid → inherits from html which has no font → default serif? Wait html class includes font vars but html doesn't set font-family. So body font-sans → var(--font-sans) invalid → body inherits from html → initial font-family (serif)! 

Hmm, that would make the ENTIRE site render in default serif... The user said the font "doesn't work" — maybe the whole site is serif? That would be very broken. But maybe the cascade actually favors the next/font classes (html class attribute), not globals.css :root. In that case everything works. The user still says broken though.

Regardless of the exact winner, these self-referential fallbacks in globals.css are WRONG and should be removed/fixed. The correct pattern in Tailwind v4 with next/font is:

```css
@theme inline {
  --font-display: var(--font-display);
  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
  --font-mono: var(--font-mono);
}
```

Wait, that's also self-referential at the `@theme` level but that's the documented Tailwind v4 + next/font pattern. In `@theme inline`, the var name maps to a Tailwind utility. The trick: inside `@theme inline`, `--font-sans: var(--font-sans)` refers to the next/font CSS variable (the one defined on html). Since `@theme` variables live in `:root` and are `inline` (expanded at use site into `font-family: var(--font-sans)`), the utility `font-sans` becomes `font-family: var(--font-sans)` where that var is the next/font one. It's a known pattern that works because the `@theme inline` block's variable isn't emitted as a real declaration on :root — wait, it is emitted. Hmm.

Actually the documented pattern from Tailwind v4 + next/font:

```css
@theme inline {
  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
}
```

This works because `@theme inline` means the value is inlined wherever the utility is used, so `font-sans` utility compiles to `font-family: var(--font-sans)` and the `var(--font-sans)` refers to the next/font variable on html — NOT to the theme variable. Without `inline`, the theme would emit `:root { --font-sans: var(--font-sans) }` causing a cycle. With `inline`, no `:root` declaration is emitted; instead the value is substituted into utilities.

But wait — in this codebase, do they use `@theme`? Let me look at globals.css fully to understand. The `.font-display` custom class is manually defined with `font-family: var(--font-display)`. If `--font-display` is properly set by next/font on html, then `.font-display` works as long as nothing overrides the var.

The self-reference in globals.css is the bug. Let me read globals.css to see the full context.

