Now I understand the font issue clearly. In the `@theme inline` block:

```css
--font-display: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif);
--font-sans: var(--font-sans, "Inter", sans-serif);
--font-mono: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", monospace);
```

`@theme inline` with `--font-display: var(--font-display)` creates a cycle because the theme variable is named the same as the CSS variable next/font provides. With `inline`, Tailwind inlines the value into utilities, so `font-display` utility would become `font-family: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif)` — wait no. With `@theme inline`, Tailwind inlines the *value* of the theme variable at usage sites. So `font-display` → `font-family: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif)`. Then `var(--font-display)` resolves to the html-level next/font variable (properly set). That's actually fine! The fallback only kicks in if the var isn't set.

BUT the problem: `@theme inline` also emits the theme variables onto `:root`? Actually for `@theme inline`, Tailwind does NOT emit the variable to `:root` (inline means "use the value directly, don't emit a CSS variable"). Hmm, wait, actually I recall `@theme inline` DOES still emit `:root` variables but ALSO inlines... No. Let me recall precisely.

Tailwind v4: By default, `@theme` variables are emitted as CSS custom properties on `:root`, and utilities reference them via `var(--...)`. With `@theme inline`, the theme value is inlined directly into the utility output instead of referencing the variable, and the variable is NOT emitted to `:root`. That's the documented pattern for next/font:

```css
@theme inline {
  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
}
```

This is the official documented pattern, so utilities like `font-sans` compile to `font-family: var(--font-sans)` referencing the next/font variable. It works.

But here they wrote `--font-display: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif)` — with a fallback. When inlined: `.font-display`... but `.font-display` is NOT a Tailwind utility — it's a hand-written class (line 105) using `var(--font-display)`. So the `@theme inline` font-display mapping is only used if a `font-display` Tailwind utility exists. Does Tailwind v4 generate a `font-display` utility from `--font-display` in `@theme`? Yes! `--font-*` namespace generates `font-*` utilities. So `--font-display` in @theme generates the `font-display` utility → `font-family: var(--font-display, "Space Grotesk", sans-serif)`.

Hmm wait, but `.font-display` hand-written class at line 105 vs the generated utility... They'd conflict but both resolve to `var(--font-display)`. 

The real problem: does Tailwind emit `--font-display` var on `:root`? With `inline` it shouldn't. But then where does the next/font variable live? On the html element via the class. So `var(--font-display)` on any element inherits the html-level next/font var. Fine.

BUT — hold on. There's a subtle conflict: next/font generates a class like `.space_grotesk__variable` that sets `--font-display: 'Space Grotesk', 'Space Grotesk Fallback'`. This class is on `<html>`. So `var(--font-display)` should work and `.font-display` should render Space Grotesk.

Hmm, so why would the font be broken? Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is `@theme inline` does NOT inline — maybe in this Tailwind version it emits `:root { --font-display: var(--font-display, ...) }` — creating a self-referential cycle on html element (html gets both the next/font class AND :root var). If `:root` declaration wins the cascade over the next/font class... specificity equal (0,1,0 both), order decides. If globals.css is injected after next/font styles, then `:root` wins and `--font-display` becomes cyclic → invalid → falls back to inherited → body font-sans → `var(--font-sans)` also cyclic → invalid → inherit → html has no font-family → initial = browser default (Times serif!). 

Actually wait — that would make the whole site serif. The user said "шрифта че не става" (the font doesn't work). This could totally be it — the whole site renders in default serif font, which looks broken.

Hmm, but actually I'm not 100% sure `@theme inline` doesn't emit vars. Let me just check what CSS is actually generated. I can inspect the compiled CSS from the build. Let me search the .next build output CSS for `--font-sans`.

Actually, the simplest robust fix regardless of the exact mechanics: don't rely on `@theme` for fonts at all, OR use different variable names to avoid the self-reference. The cleanest approach that definitely works:

Option A: Remove `--font-*` from `@theme inline` entirely, and define the font vars directly on `:root` and `html.dark` etc. But next/font already defines `--font-display`/`--font-sans`/`--font-mono` on html via its class. The hand-written `.font-display` and `body { font-family: var(--font-sans) }` reference them directly. So the simplest fix: DELETE lines 81-83 from globals.css. Then:
- `var(--font-sans)` in body → resolves to next/font var on html → Inter. ✓
- `.font-display` → `var(--font-display)` → Space Grotesk. ✓
- But do any Tailwind `font-sans`/`font-mono` utilities get used in the codebase? Yes: `<body className="... font-sans ...">` uses `font-sans`. Without `--font-sans` in `@theme`, Tailwind v4's default `--font-sans` theme var exists (it has a default font stack). So `font-sans` utility → default Tailwind font stack (ui-sans-serif, system-ui...). And `font-mono` utilities → default mono stack. That's fine-ish but the body uses `font-sans` class AND `font-family: var(--font-sans)` via CSS. The class `font-sans` (utility) would set font-family to Tailwind default stack, overriding the body CSS `font-family: var(--font-sans)`. Hmm, specificity: both are same, source order in generated CSS decides. Messy.

Better Option B: keep `@theme inline` but rename the underlying references to next/font variables to avoid collision. next/font lets you choose the variable names. In layout.tsx the variables are `--font-display`, `--font-sans`, `--font-mono`. Keep those as the *source* vars. Then in `@theme inline`, map the Tailwind font namespace to DIFFERENT internal names so there's no self-reference. E.g.:

```css
@theme inline {
  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
  ...
}
```

Hmm that's the documented pattern though — Tailwind's official docs literally show:

```css
@theme inline {
  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
}
```

Wait, that IS self-referential at the variable level. How does the official pattern work? Because with `@theme inline`, the emitted output for the `font-sans` utility is `font-family: var(--font-sans)` — where the utility inlines the theme value `var(--font-sans)`. And the theme var `--font-sans` itself is NOT emitted to `:root` (that's the whole point of `inline`). So the `var(--font-sans)` inside the utility refers to the next/font variable. No cycle because there's no `:root { --font-sans: ... }` declaration emitted.

So the pattern DOES work when Tailwind doesn't emit the theme var. The question is whether this Tailwind version emits it. Given the user says the font is broken, maybe the version emits `:root` anyway (changing behavior), or maybe the issue is different.

Let me just empirically check the compiled CSS. Let me search the .next static css for `--font-sans`.

Actually, dev mode uses runtime CSS. Let me fetch the CSS from the dev server. In dev, the CSS is served from /_next/static/chunks/...css. I saw in the account page HTML: `<link rel="stylesheet" href="/_next/static/chunks/[root-of-the-server]__06oh1o8._.css">`. Let me fetch it and grep for --font-sans.

Let me do that.

